<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=clay-tokens">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clay tokens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 8000–7500 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clay tokens are small shaped pieces of clay that stood for animals and goods, first used to keep records in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a> around 8000–7500 BCE; the earliest known come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. For trade they were sealed in hollow clay balls, or bullae, to prevent deception. Marks impressed and later drawn on clay tablets replaced them, and around 3100 BCE, when signs for numbers appeared, the tokens gave way to pictographic tablets, the beginning of Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clay-tokens.jpg" alt="Clay tokens" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clay_accounting_tokens_Susa_Louvre_n1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 8000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>The Zagros, Iran</dd></div></dl><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Agriculture<span>c. 10,000 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform<span>c. 3300 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=clay-tokens">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
